{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>Events & Listeners</h1>
    <p>
        Rhapsody includes a simple but powerful event dispatcher system that allows you to decouple various aspects of your application. By dispatching events, you can allow other parts of your application, or "listeners," to react to an action without the original code needing to know about them. This is a great way to handle side effects, like sending a notification email after a user registers.
    </p>
    <p>
        This approach follows the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Observer_pattern" target="_blank">Observer design pattern</a> and leads to a more modular and maintainable codebase.
    </p>

    <h2>The Workflow at a Glance</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>An action occurs in your application (e.g., a user registers).</li>
        <li>Your controller creates and **dispatches** an `Event` object containing data about what happened.</li>
        <li>The `EventDispatcher` receives the event and finds all registered **Listeners** for it.</li>
        <li>The dispatcher executes the `handle()` method on each listener, passing them the event object.</li>
    </ol>
    <hr>
    
    <h2>1. Creating Events and Listeners</h2>
    <p>The Rhapsody console includes generator commands to quickly create new event and listener classes for your application.</p>
    
    <h3>Creating an Event</h3>
    <p>This command will create a new event class in the `app/Events` directory. Events are simple data objects that hold information about the event.</p>
    <pre class="command-line"><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody make:event UserRegistered</code></pre>
    <p>You can then edit the generated `UserRegistered.php` file to accept data in its constructor, like the user object.</p>
    
    <h3>Creating a Listener</h3>
    <p>This command creates a new listener class in the `app/Listeners` directory. Listeners contain the logic to handle an event.</p>
    <pre class="command-line"><code class="language-bash">php rhapsody make:listener SendWelcomeEmail</code></pre>
    <p>You will then implement the logic for sending the email inside the `handle()` method of the generated `SendWelcomeEmail.php` file.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>2. Registering Listeners</h2>
    <p>
        Once you have created your event and listener classes, you need to tell the framework that the listener should be executed when the event is dispatched. This is done in the `app/Providers/EventServiceProvider.php` file.
    </p>
    <p>
        The `$listen` property is an array where the keys are the event classes and the values are arrays of listener classes that should handle them.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">
// in app/Providers/EventServiceProvider.php
use App\Events\UserRegistered;
use App\Listeners\SendWelcomeEmail;

class EventServiceProvider
{
    protected array $listen = [
        // When the UserRegistered event is dispatched...
        UserRegistered::class => [
            // ...execute the SendWelcomeEmail listener.
            SendWelcomeEmail::class,
        ],
    ];
    // ...
}
</code></pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>3. Dispatching Events</h2>
    <p>
        The final step is to dispatch the event from your controller (or any other class). You can do this by injecting the `Core\Events\EventDispatcher` into your controller's constructor and calling its `dispatch()` method.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">
// in app/Controllers/AuthController.php
use App\Events\UserRegistered;
use Rhapsody\Core\Events\EventDispatcher;

class AuthController extends BaseController
{
    public function __construct(
        protected EventDispatcher $dispatcher,
        // ... other dependencies
    ) {
        // ...
    }
    
    public function register(Request $request): Response
    {
        // ... validation and user creation logic ...

        // After the user is saved, dispatch the event
        $this->dispatcher->dispatch(new UserRegistered($user));
        
        // ... redirect the user ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
    <p>
        Now, your controller's only responsibility is to handle the registration. It no longer knows anything about sending emails. You can easily add more listeners (e.g., `LogUserRegistration`, `UpdateUserStats`) to the `EventServiceProvider` in the future without ever having to change your `AuthController` again.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2 id="stoppable-events">4. Stoppable Events (Advanced)</h2>
    <p>
        Everything above describes the normal way events work in Rhapsody: dispatching an event is <strong>fire-and-forget</strong>. The code that calls <code>dispatch()</code> doesn't wait to see what listeners do, and nothing a listener does can change what happens next. This is true for <code>UserRegistered</code>, and it's true for almost every event you'll ever write — the whole point of events is to let listeners react to something without the original code needing to know or care.
    </p>
    <p>
        A very small number of events in the framework break that rule on purpose: they let a listener supply an answer that the dispatching code actually uses. The framework ships one example of this, <code>Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound</code>, which the router dispatches right before it gives up and shows a 404 page — see the <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/routing">Routing</a> documentation for that specific example. This section explains the general mechanism behind it, in case you ever need to build something similar for your own application.
    </p>

    <h3>The StoppableEventInterface</h3>
    <p>
        An event opts into this behavior by implementing <code>Rhapsody\Core\Events\StoppableEventInterface</code>, which requires a single method:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">interface StoppableEventInterface
{
    public function isPropagationStopped(): bool;
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Inside <code>EventDispatcher::dispatch()</code>, after every listener runs, the dispatcher asks two questions: does this event implement <code>StoppableEventInterface</code>, and if so, is <code>isPropagationStopped()</code> now <code>true</code>? If the answer to both is yes, the dispatcher stops calling any remaining listeners and hands the event straight back to whoever called <code>dispatch()</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Important:</strong> this check only ever applies to events that implement the interface. <code>UserRegistered</code> and every other ordinary event you create do not implement it, so they are completely unaffected by any of this — every listener always runs for them, in the order they were registered, exactly as described in the sections above.
    </p>

    <h3>Example: RouteNotFound</h3>
    <p>
        <code>RouteNotFound</code> is a good concrete example to study, and you can use it directly rather than building your own. It carries the unmatched URI and HTTP method, plus a place for a listener to put a response:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">class RouteNotFound extends Event implements StoppableEventInterface
{
    private ?Response $response = null;
    private bool $propagationStopped = false;

    public function __construct(
        public readonly string $uri,
        public readonly string $method,
    ) {}

    // A listener calls this when it has resolved the URI to something real.
    public function setResponse(Response $response): void
    {
        $this->response = $response;
        $this->propagationStopped = true; // setting a response always stops propagation
    }

    public function getResponse(): ?Response
    {
        return $this->response;
    }

    public function isPropagationStopped(): bool
    {
        return $this->propagationStopped;
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Notice that <code>setResponse()</code> is the <em>only</em> way to mark the event as stopped. You can't accidentally stop propagation without also providing a response, and you can't end up with two different responses from two different listeners — whichever listener calls <code>setResponse()</code> first wins, and no listener registered after it gets a turn.
    </p>

    <h3>Writing a Listener for a Stoppable Event</h3>
    <p>
        A listener for a stoppable event is written exactly like any other listener (see <strong>1. Creating Events and Listeners</strong> above). The only difference is in what it does inside <code>handle()</code>: if it finds a match, it calls <code>setResponse()</code>; if it doesn't, it simply returns and does nothing, leaving the event unstopped so the next listener — or the framework's normal fallback behavior — gets a chance.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">namespace App\Listeners;

use Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound;
use Rhapsody\Core\Response;

class RedirectLegacyUrls
{
    public function handle(RouteNotFound $event): void
    {
        // Look up $event->uri in your own table of old-slug -> new-slug redirects.
        $newPath = $this->findRedirectFor($event->uri);

        if ($newPath === null) {
            return; // no match — let the next listener try, or fall through to the 404
        }

        $response = new Response();
        $response->setStatusCode(301);
        $response->setHeader('Location', $newPath);

        $event->setResponse($response);
    }

    private function findRedirectFor(string $uri): ?string
    {
        // your own lookup logic here
        return null;
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>
        You register this listener for <code>RouteNotFound</code> the same way you'd register any other listener — see <strong>2. Registering Listeners</strong> above. If you're writing this inside a marketplace module rather than the main application, remember that a module also needs to declare <code>Rhapsody\Core\Events\RouteNotFound</code> in its <code>events.listen.listen</code> permission list in <code>module.json</code> before it's allowed to listen for the event at all — a module that omits this will get a <code>ModulePermissionException</code> instead.
    </p>
